          "derivation": "The Avestan half is exact: Bartholomae's Altiranisches Wörterbuch has gaēsa- m. 'Lockenhaar' (curly hair) and the adjective gaēsav- 'kraus', 'lockenhaarig', extended 'zottelhaarig' of a camel (uštrahe … gaēsauš) — Nourai's 'gaĕsa, gaĕsu' are these two, with the -u- stem gaēsu- being the compositional/adjectival form Pokorny quotes. The second pair, however, is NOT Old Persian: no such word is attested in the Achaemenid inscriptions (Kent's lexicon has nothing), and 'ges, gesuk' are the Middle Persian forms — MacKenzie's Concise Pahlavi Dictionary p.36 'ges [gys | (M gyswg, N gesu)] curls, locks', i.e. Book-Pahlavi gēs with the Manichaean-script variant gēsūg. Phonologically Av. gaēsa- > MP gēs by the regular monophthongisation of Old Iranian ai > MP ē and loss of the thematic ending, while gēsūg is gēs + the MP diminutive/adjectival suffix -ūg (< OIr. -uka-). The derivation drawn is therefore right, the language label is not: the node lumps Avestan with 'Old Persian' where it should read Middle Persian (Pahlavi).",
